      <div set:html={`
<div>
<h2 id="news-hook">Library Ebooks Just Got Easier — and More Complicated</h2>

<p>Two stories hit the e-reader world within 24 hours this week. On August 15, <a href="https://www.theverge.com/tech/976390/xteink-x3-x4-x4-pro-e-reader-libby-plug-ins-drm-crosspoint-reader" rel="noopener">The Verge reported</a> that Xteink's tiny pocket e-readers are finally getting access to books borrowed through OverDrive's Libby library app. One day earlier, MakeUseOf reported that Kindle's latest update might break the Libby trick millions of readers depend on.</p>

<p>If you have ever borrowed an ebook from your library and struggled to get it onto the device you want to read on, this is the article for you. Below is the complete 2026 state of library ebooks on e-readers: native Libby support, the workarounds, what just changed for Xteink owners, and a universal EPUB workflow that works on anything.</p>

<h2 id="what-is-libby">What Is Libby and How Does It Work?</h2>

<p>Libby is the mobile and desktop app for <a href="https://libbyapp.com/" rel="noopener">borrowing digital books from your public library</a>. It is built by OverDrive, which supplies digital lending to more than 90 percent of North American public libraries — <a href="https://mashable.com/article/libby-app-how-to-read-free-kindle-e-books" rel="noopener">Mashable's e-reader expert</a> puts the figure higher — and Libby works in 78 countries. Sign in with your library card and you get instant access to its digital collection: ebooks, audiobooks, and magazines, all free.</p>

<p>The lending model is worth understanding. Libraries buy licensed digital copies of each title, so popular new books usually have a waitlist — Libby lets you place a hold and notifies you when a copy is ready. Loans last up to 21 days depending on your library's rules, some titles can be renewed, and books return automatically, so there are no late fees. In September 2025, Libby changed its hold system so readers suspend a hold until they are ready for it instead of delaying it — a change that upset some users but keeps books moving.</p>

<p>A pro tip: you can attach multiple library cards to one Libby account. Different networks have different catalogs and wait times, so comparing a title's availability across two or three cards is often the fastest way to get a popular book.</p>

<h2 id="why-epub">Why Library Books Are Always EPUB</h2>

<p>Here is the fact that makes library ebooks relevant to this site: <strong>library ebooks are EPUB files.</strong> OverDrive lends books in the open EPUB standard (plus a separate audiobook format for audio), protected with Adobe Digital Editions DRM so they expire when the loan ends. When you "download" a borrowed book from Libby, what you actually receive is a tiny <em>.acsm</em> file — an Adobe Content Server Message that a compatible reading app or device uses to fetch the encrypted EPUB and unlock it for the loan period.</p>

<p>That matters because EPUB is the format the rest of the publishing world uses and the format converter-epub.com exists to produce. A library EPUB is a proper reflowable ebook: fonts resize, margins adjust, and chapters navigate cleanly. Any device or app that handles Adobe DRM EPUB files can read your library books — which is why device support looks the way it does below.</p>

<h2 id="kindle">Read Libby Books on Kindle</h2>

<p>The Kindle gets most of the attention in library borrowing — and most of the friction. In the US, Libby offers a "Read With Kindle" option: borrow a book, tap that button, and Amazon's site shows a "Get Library Book" button. The book then appears in your Kindle library, where you download it like any other title. The catch is that the integration only works with US libraries and US Amazon accounts; outside the US, the button does not exist.</p>

<p>Kindle owners in the US have also long relied on a trick to keep borrowed books after the loan expires: download the book, then switch the Kindle to airplane mode so it cannot see the return notice. On August 14, MakeUseOf reported that Amazon's latest Kindle software update might break that hack, which the outlet says millions of readers depend on. The report was based on the rollout and reader reports rather than an official Amazon announcement, but it is a reminder that the airplane-mode workaround is a fragile convenience, not a feature.</p>

<p>If your loans keep expiring and you want a more robust path, the alternatives below — especially the EPUB workflow at the end — sidestep the Kindle hacks entirely. And if you are weighing a switch, our <a href="/blog/kobo-vs-kindle-vs-pocketbook-2026/">Kobo vs Kindle vs PocketBook comparison</a> covers how the platforms differ.</p>

<h2 id="kobo">Read Libby Books on Kobo</h2>

<p>Kobo is the smoothest Libby experience on a dedicated e-reader. Kobo devices have OverDrive built in: sign in once, and every book you borrow in Libby automatically appears on the device, ready to download. No cables, no ACSM files, no extra steps. As The Verge put it, Kobo's native OverDrive support remains the reference point every other implementation is compared against.</p>

<p>The trade-off is that a Kobo can only have one OverDrive account attached at a time, which matters if you use multiple library cards. Mashable's recommendation: pick the <strong>Kobo Libra Colour</strong> for a single card (zero-friction borrowing) and the <strong>Kindle Paperwhite</strong> if you juggle several cards. Kobo's retail momentum — including its <a href="/blog/kobo-best-buy-us-stores-2026/">return to Best Buy stores in August 2026</a> — makes that easier to act on than ever.</p>

<h2 id="pocketbook">Read Libby Books on PocketBook</h2>

<p>PocketBook has been quietly building out its own native Libby support. After integrating Libby with its newer devices in late 2025, PocketBook extended the integration to older e-readers in 2026 — Good e-Reader reported in April that older PocketBook models now get Libby as well. On a supported PocketBook, you sign into your library account through the device and borrowed books sync over, much like the Kobo experience.</p>

<p>PocketBook's appeal is broader than Libby: the devices read EPUB, MOBI, FB2, and a dozen other formats natively — a natural fit for anyone building a <a href="/blog/drm-free-epub-library-2026/">DRM-free EPUB library</a> alongside their library loans.</p>

<h2 id="boox-android">Read Libby Books on Boox and Android E-Ink</h2>

<p>E-ink tablets from Onyx Boox, Meebook, and other Android-based manufacturers have no built-in OverDrive integration, but they do not need one: you install the Libby app from the Google Play store and read directly inside it, or you use Adobe Digital Editions to open borrowed EPUBs on the device. Mashable's guide notes that Boox e-readers can access borrowed books through Adobe Digital Editions — the same ADE authorization the Xteink plug-in below relies on — even though the "tedious steps" make Boox a less convenient choice for pure library reading than Kobo or Kindle.</p>

<p>The payoff of the Android route is flexibility: a Boox device runs the full Libby app with search, holds, and audiobooks. If you already own one, the <a href="/blog/instapaper-boox-palma-2026/">Instapaper integration on the Boox Palma</a> shows how far these devices have come as reading companions.</p>

<h2 id="xteink">Xteink Just Got Libby Access (August 2026)</h2>

<p>The big news this week is for Xteink owners. Xteink's X3, X4, and X4 Pro are the viral "purse Kindle" pocket readers that cost $79–$149 — but until now their biggest weakness was sourcing books. The devices have no bookstore, and stock firmware made even side-loading a chore. As The Verge's Andrew Liszewski explained on August 15, that is changing thanks to <a href="https://crosspointreader.com/" rel="noopener">CrossPoint Reader</a>, a free open-source alternative firmware.</p>

<p>CrossPoint Reader is adding third-party plug-in support, and the first community plug-in — <strong>Protected Content</strong> — unlocks DRM-protected ebooks borrowed through Libby or purchased from stores like Kobo that use Adobe DRM. It is still in beta, but the developers provided The Verge with a build that worked, and it is now available to anyone.</p>

<p>Here is what the setup looks like, based on The Verge's hands-on testing on an X4 Pro:</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Flash CrossPoint Reader.</strong> Connect your Xteink e-reader over USB-C and flash the free firmware through a browser using the CrossPoint Reader website, selecting your device and firmware version.</li>
  <li><strong>Install the Plugin Store.</strong> Unzip a few files onto the e-reader's microSD card, which adds a Plugins section to the home screen.</li>
  <li><strong>Install Protected Content.</strong> This plug-in runs through CrossPoint Reader's built-in web server and unlocks Adobe DRM ebooks.</li>
  <li><strong>Authorize with a ByteBooks or Adobe Digital Editions account.</strong> You need a free ByteBooks account, or you can convert an old ADE account you have not touched in years, so the e-reader can open DRM-protected books.</li>
  <li><strong>Borrow in Libby on another device.</strong> Because Xteink devices run on low-power ESP-32 processors, there is no on-device Libby search. Borrow the book in the Libby app, download the ACSM file, and move it to the device wirelessly through CrossPoint Reader's web server.</li>
  <li><strong>Read for the loan period.</strong> The plug-in processes the ACSM file, fetches the EPUB, and copies it to your e-reader, where it stays readable for as long as the loan allows.</li>
</ol>

<p>The same process works for EPUB books you bought from Kobo's website: download the ACSM file from your Kobo account and process it with the plug-in. And for an even simpler path, a free app called <strong>Common Stacks</strong> (Android, Windows, and macOS, with iOS coming soon) provides full access to Libby and can send borrowed books straight to Xteink e-readers with a couple of clicks. The Verge's tester said it was as easy as borrowing on a Kobo.</p>

<h2 id="comparison">Libby on E-Readers: Comparison Table</h2>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Device</th><th>How Libby books get on it</th><th>Effort</th><th>Key caveat</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><strong>Kindle</strong></td><td>Read With Kindle (US only) → Get Library Book → download</td><td>Medium — one extra step</td><td>US-only integration; airplane-mode hack reportedly at risk after the latest update</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Kobo</strong></td><td>Built-in OverDrive sign-in; books auto-sync</td><td>Lowest</td><td>One OverDrive account per device</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>PocketBook</strong></td><td>Native Libby integration (2025–2026 rollout)</td><td>Low</td><td>Support depends on model and region</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Boox / Android e-ink</strong></td><td>Libby app, or Adobe Digital Editions</td><td>Medium</td><td>Reading inside an app, not a native bookshelf</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Xteink X3 / X4 / X4 Pro</strong></td><td>CrossPoint Reader + Protected Content plug-in (beta), or Common Stacks app</td><td>High first time, then easy</td><td>Needs firmware flash + ADE authorization; no on-device Libby search</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="universal-workflow">The Universal EPUB Workflow for Library Books</h2>

<p>If you own a device not in the list above — or just want a method that is not tied to any brand — there is a universal workflow built on EPUB and Adobe DRM, the backbone of library ebook reading for a decade.</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Borrow the book in Libby</strong> and choose the download option (not Read With Kindle). Libby gives you an <em>.acsm</em> file.</li>
  <li><strong>Authorize a reader with Adobe Digital Editions</strong> — the ADE desktop app, a supported e-reader, or the <a href="https://calibre-ebook.com/" rel="noopener">Calibre</a> DeACSM plug-in, which imports the borrowed EPUB into your library.</li>
  <li><strong>Convert if needed.</strong> Because the book is now an EPUB, use Calibre's conversion tools to reshape it for any device — the official <a href="https://manual.calibre-ebook.com/conversion.html" rel="noopener">Calibre conversion documentation</a> covers the settings. This is the same pipeline as our <a href="/blog/calibre-epub-to-kindle-workflow-2026/">Calibre-to-Kindle guide</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Side-load to your device</strong> over USB or wirelessly, read for the loan period, and let the loan expire automatically. When the book returns itself, it simply disappears from your reading app.</li>
</ol>

<p>The beauty of this method is that it treats library books as what they are: EPUB files with an expiration date. The borrowed EPUB behaves exactly like any other ebook in your pipeline, so the same tricks apply — turning <a href="/blog/kindle-to-epub/">Kindle files into EPUB</a>, <a href="/blog/send-epub-to-kindle/">sending EPUB to a Kindle</a>, or building a <a href="/blog/drm-free-epub-library-2026/">clean personal EPUB library</a>.</p>

